upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7f/Mary_Celeste_NYTimes_1873February26.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mary_Celeste_NYTimes_1873February26.png","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=6093396","extmetadata":{"Categories":{"value":"PD US|The Boston Post|Mary Celeste (ship, 1861)|February 1873|The New York Times, 1873|1873-02-26|PD-US missing SDC copyright status","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>\"A Brig's Officers Believed to Have Been Murdered at Sea.\", <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Times\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:New York Times\">New York Times</a></i>, Wednesday, February 26, 1873, page 2, citing the <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boston_Post\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Boston Post\">Boston Post</a></i> of February 24, 1873. Retrieved on June 19, 2008.\n</p>\nFirst sentence: \"It is now believed that the fine brig <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Celeste\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Mary Celeste\">Mary Celeste</a>, of about 236 tons, commanded by Capt. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Briggs\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Benjamin Briggs\">Benjamin Briggs</a>, of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marion,_Massachusetts\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Marion, Massachusetts\">Marion, Mass.</a>, was seized by pirates in the latter part of November, and that, after murdering the Captain, his wife, child, and officers, the vessel was abandoned near the Western Islands, where the miscreants are supposed to have landed.\"","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boston_Post\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Boston Post\">Boston Post</a></i>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"Restrictions":{"value